    "result_text": "Tests are green (2 run, 0 failures/errors), `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\n## What I changed\n\n**Agreed with the SHA-256 duplication finding** and addressed it:\n\n- Added `util/HashUtils.java` with a single `sha256Hex(String)` returning the full lower-case hex digest \u2014 the one home for the digest\u2192hex loop and its `NoSuchAlgorithmException` handling, following the existing `TelephoneFormatter` util convention (final class, private ctor, same license header).\n- `Owner.getIdentityKey()` now calls `HashUtils.sha256Hex(...)`; removed the entity's private `sha256Hex` copy and its now-unused `MessageDigest`/`StandardCharsets`/`NoSuchAlgorithmException` imports.\n- `OwnerRestControllerV1.hashHex` is now a one-liner delegating to `HashUtils.sha256Hex(input).substring(0, length).toUpperCase(...)`, preserving its upper-case/truncate contract for `customerCode` and `householdId`; removed the controller's now-unused crypto imports and added the `HashUtils` import.\n\nAll three SHA-256 call sites now share one implementation, and the model no longer reaches into the controller.\n\n## Left as-is (minor, non-blocking)\n\nThe Soundex methods stay as private statics on `Owner`. The reviewer themselves called this defensible since only `Owner` consumes them, and said they'd move it only if the util extraction gave it an *obvious* home. `HashUtils` is a hashing-specific helper \u2014 folding an unrelated phonetic-encoding algorithm into it (or inventing a util whose sole consumer is one entity) would be a worse fit than leaving it beside the `getLastNameSoundex()` getter that uses it. If a second consumer appears, a dedicated `codec`/`Soundex` util would be the time to extract it.",
