        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe change is largely well-placed: the identity-key derivation stays on the `Owner` entity alongside the existing `getIdentityKey()` and the other derived `@Transient` getters (`getTimezone`, `getContactPreference`, `getAgeBand`), and the two controller methods (`rejectDuplicateIdentity`, `applyPossibleDuplicate`) are edited in place rather than spawning a new catch-all. The new `getLastNameSoundex()` getter mirrors that existing convention naturally, and `soundex`/`soundexDigit` are consumed only by `Owner`, so they belong where they are.\n\nOne concrete structural issue:\n\n**Duplicated SHA-256 hex logic across two classes.** The new `Owner.sha256Hex` (`Owner.java:557`) is a verbatim copy of the digest\u2192hex loop already present in `OwnerRestControllerV1.hashHex` (`OwnerRestControllerV1.java:703`):\n\n```java\nbyte[] digest = MessageDigest.getInstance(\"SHA-256\").digest(input.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));\nStringBuilder hex = new StringBuilder(digest.length * 2);\nfor (byte b : digest) { hex.append(String.format(\"%02x\", b)); }\n...\ncatch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) { throw new IllegalStateException(\"SHA-256 algorithm not available\", e); }\n```\n\nWith this change there are now three SHA-256 call sites (`customerCode` hash8 at line 642, `computeHouseholdId` at line 930, and the new `getIdentityKey`) split across two classes with two independent copies of the same loop and the same exception handling. The only real difference is the output contract \u2014 the controller's variant upper-cases and truncates to `length`, while the model's returns the full lower-case digest.\n\nSuggested fix: extract a single shared helper (e.g. a small `HashUtils.sha256Hex(String)` returning the full lower-case hex) and have the controller's `hashHex` call it and then `.substring(0, length).toUpperCase(...)`. That removes the duplicated digest/exception-handling block, gives the hashing one home, and leaves `getIdentityKey` able to depend on it without the model reaching into the controller. This is justified by multiple call sites rather than speculative abstraction \u2014 it's not over-engineering for a one-off.\n\nMinor (non-blocking): a full Soundex implementation living as private static methods on a JPA entity is a little unusual for a Spring codebase; a `codec`/util home (or Apache Commons Codec's `Soundex`) would read more naturally. Since only `Owner` consumes it today, keeping it on the entity is defensible \u2014 I'd only move it if the shared-util extraction above gives it an obvious home."
